# mm.audio.es8311

The Everest Semiconductor ES8311 audio codec, its DAC as an mm.audio Out, over
portable mm.mcu I2C and I2S. It contains no vendor header and no board pin
number: a board's provider supplies the wiring, constructs a Codec, and
registers it as the board's Speaker. It sits where mm.imu.qmi8658 sits for the
IMU, because the ES8311 is a chip with registers worth writing once.

The codec is identified by its two chip identification registers, 0x83 and
0x11 at 0xfd and 0xfe, and a part that answers otherwise is Unsupported. It is
controlled at 0x18, or 0x19 with CE high. initialize resets it, powers up its
analog section, DAC, and output driver at unity volume, and leaves the DAC's
serial port muted. configure starts the I2S link as the platform's slave and
programs the clock manager to take the codec's master clock from the bit clock,
multiplied to 256 times the rate, so a board needs no MCLK line. start unmutes
the port and stop mutes it before discarding what was queued.

It keeps the Out contract: service offers its pending run first and reads from
the Stream only when nothing is pending, the transmitter's silence on underrun
is reported to the Stream, and position and pending come from the
transmitter's progress. A mono sample goes to both slots. Slots are sixteen or
thirty-two bits wide; the sixteen-bit link moves samples unchanged.

The register values follow the datasheet's map as Espressif's driver programs
it for this mode. The first version of this driver, on the audio-work branch,
addressed the part at 0x06, programmed the ADC's serial port 0x0a where the
DAC's is 0x09, and never enabled the clock manager, the analog section, or the
DAC; each is corrected here. The ADC and microphone path is absent, because
this module is an Out. None of this is qualified on hardware until a board with
the codec plays through it.
